d it really does incentivize them to  repay that money back  more quickly right our senior business  correspondent ann gaviola in toronto  a canadian billionaire and entrepreneur  who built the family business into a  food and retailing empire  has died his family says galen weston  senior died peacefully at his home on  monday after a long illness  the patriarch of the western empire he  took over and turned around  loblaws and went on to acquire multiple  businesses including holt renfrew  and selfridge's group he was also a  philanthropist who donated to a range of  organizations across canada  westin is being remembered as a beloved  husband a proud father and a loving  brother  he was married to former ontario  lieutenant governor hillary weston for  55 years they  raised two children his son galen jr now  ceo of both loblaws and george weston  company  says his father's greatest gift was  inspiring others to achieve  more than they thought possible galen  weston senior was 80 years old  big cuts at a canadian university coming  up dozens of layoffs and programs  lost will it save the school  we think of universities as pillars of  higher learning that once established  just keep going strong but  what's happened at laurentian university  in sudbury ontario has shaken that idea  to the core  yesterday dozens of professors were  fired and multiple  undergrad and graduate programs were cut  eric sorensen explains why  and what a shudder it is sent through  other universities  the deserted campus and the empty  classrooms a consequence of the pandemic  at post-secondary schools everywhere  tells only part of the story at one  university in northern ontario  laurentian hit such dire financial  straits that two months ago it became  the first university in the country  ever to seek creditor protection from  the courts now it is shutting down  programs and laying off professors  like nadia vareli who says she was  blindsided political science was cut  only because political science is one of  the oldest uh  disciplines in the social sciences so  there's a lot of  confusion of anger and a lot of hurt in  all 69 programs including one-third of  undergraduate programs are being closed  from music to environmental studies 34  english language programs are out  as well as 24 french language programs  and eleven  graduate programs for master's degrees  are being discontinued  caitlyn cotella has been a student for  four years it's a  heartbreaking announcement when you're  told that something that you've worked  so hard for  is being ripped away from you at no  fault of your own  laurentian is bilingual with emphasis on  french language and indigenous studies  as well as english  and represents a beacon of higher  education in one of the northern less  populated regions of the country  businesses will close people's  plans for for education in northern  ontario will be disrupted  the ontario government defended its  policies but would say little else as  laurentian goes through creditor  protection  this government remains resolute in  ensuring pathways to graduation for all  students one reason all universities are  hurting is a reliance on foreign  students  who pay much higher tuition costs but  have stayed home during the pandemic  this government has decided to  prioritize using international students  essentially as cash cows to uphold its  entire post-secondary education system  since 2018 we've seen a steady decrease  in public funding for these institutions  and that is what we need to focus on it  is all provincial jurisdiction but the  prime minister says ottawa will weigh  in with ontario to see what can be done  particularly at a bilingual institution  with a focus on minority communities  eric sorensen global news toronto  toronto's interim police chief has  apologized to the city's lgbtq2  community  an independent review found systemic  discrimination was a factor in the  forces handling of several missing  persons cases  the review called for truly  transformative change in the force it  was ordered  after the arrest of bruce mcarthur who  pleaded guilty to murdering eight men  who had gone missing all had connections  to the city's gay village  the report found some officers had  misconceptions or  stereotypical ideas that impeded their  work chief james ramer says toronto  police will act on all 151  recommendations  none of this is acceptable none of this  should have happened  as we all know tragically lives were  lost  to the family friends and community  members left behind  we apologize you deserve better and we  deeply regret the impact  our actions and inactions had and  continue to have  on your lives the chief says toronto  police will double the number of missing  persons investigators he says the  department is exploring how to integrate  civilian support workers into the  missing persons unit  more anger over the shooting of dante  right ahead the resignations  from the police force  you're watching global national  hundreds of protesters defied a dust to  dawn curfew in brooklyn center minnesota  overnight  angry about the police shooting of an  unarmed black man during a traffic stop  the white officer who shot 20-year-old  dante wright and the police chief both  resigned today  the chief of said has said the officer  kim potter mistakenly grabbed her gun  instead of her taser today wright's  mother revealed the last time she saw  her son was  shortly after he was shot when the woman  in the car with him  picked up his phone then she pointed the  phone towards the driver's seat and my  son was laying there  unresponsive that was the last time that  i seen my son  that's the last time i heard from my son  and i have had no explanation since then  next what a new study reveals about  suicide risks during the pandemic  you've never had to think probably about  how you'd rescue a giraffe  well this is how this calf was the last  of nine giraffes stranded on an island  in kenya  rising lake waters repeatedly flooded  their habitat  they're a highly endangered subspecies  called rothschild's giraffes and it took  15 months to plan how to tranquilize  blindfold and ferry them off the island  to a nearby sanctuary that custom-built  barge by the way is called a  g-raft well we're all living through  tough times right now covet-19 has taken  a tremendous toll  drug overdose deaths have increased as  has demand for mental health support  but an international study found no  increase in suicides at least in the  first months of the pandemic  heather urex west explores the possible  reasons why  and what it means for the road ahead  last  june when the world was in the grip of  covin 19's first  wave andrea gordon lost her partner  david to suicide  david felt very isolated he was a very  social person and he  really missed his friends and his family  and the ability to go out and  work full-time all of that was curtailed  for him  all of that was adding to his anxiety  and adding to  his worries about the future gordon  doesn't know if the pandemic was  entirely to blame for her partner's  death but she believes it had an impact  though a new international study  published in the lancet psychiatry  has found that at least in the first  months of covet 19  in many parts of the world suicide rates  did not rise  the research tracked the number of  suicides in 21 countries including  canada  between april 1 and july 31 2020  it found no evidence the pandemic caused  an increased risk of suicide  in fact in 12 areas including bc and  alberta suicide rates actually declined  there was a 24 reduction in suicides in  british columbia  and a 20 reduction in alberta but just  because suicide rates did not increase  in the early days of covet 19 doesn't  mean we're out of the woods yet  for many people stress levels increase  the longer this pandemic drags on  and there will also be long-term mental  health impacts to consider particularly  for health care workers  who will likely deal with post-traumatic  stress long after covert 19  is resolved suicide is often a lagging  indicator  so when something awful happens we don't  typically see suicide go up  we often see a coming together effect  but as time goes on  and people exhaust their personal  resources  life can become more and more bleak i  sat on the advisory committee for that  it's why andrea gordon is now working as  a suicide prevention  advocate sharing her partner's story in  the hope's  other lives can be saved heather urex  west global news  calgary if you or someone you know needs  support there is help including through  crisis services canada  you can call 1-833-456  or text 45645  that is global national for this tuesday  i'm donna friesen  tonight's your canada is the now covid  compliant  lunch break sculpture in downtown  edmonton  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  [Music]  [Applause]  bye-bye  you
 

